</ul><h1>T2B: Time To Boogy</h1><p>The PartySPKR! You know it’s good for parties because its creators were so busy partying, they forgot to add a space and the second word’s vowels!</p>
<p>But what makes it a “party speaker”? It’s simple: the  8 light settings, which include solid colors and rainbow modes! Okay, but seriously, this is one of those features that likely will make you roll your eyes… until you actually use it, and then you’ll realize it’s pretty rad.</p>
<p>But the other thing that makes it a party speaker? The shape.</p>
<p>Look, there are plenty of companies making unassuming little cylinders that pump out great sound. And all of theoe would do just fine providing the soundtrack to your various shindigs.</p>
<p>But there’s something more fun about a big, aggressive-looking boombox, right? You walk in and set this thing down, and people will think: <em>Oh, shit! It’s time to dance!</em></p>
<p>And it has plenty of power to accommodate this, as well. According to the manufacturer’s copy, it provides:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>powerful 60 W RMS audio with dual tweeters, dual woofers, dual passive radiators, and dual amplifiers for rich, room-filling sound.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If we have one quibble there, it’s that last bit. “Room-filling sound”? Buddy, it’s Mid-May, AKA the official kick-off of outdoor get-together season. Don’t fill a room with this thing. Fill THE SKY with music!</p>
<p>Or not. You can also use it to listen to a podcast while you prep veggies for dinner.</p>
<p>Or hey, why not use it for both at the same time? Listen to podcasts and music by pairing it with your laptop and playing some music and a podcast simultaneously. Heck, you might create something amazing, like:</p>
<ul>
<li>Radiolabba</li>
<li>2 Dope Queen</li>
<li>Neu! Heights</li>
<li>Telepathy Tapes ‘n Tapes</li>
<li>You’re Cory Wong About</li>
